From 1a0053948e36b6dd355eb416e8d855ef63c5865f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1vivy <1vivy@tutanota.com>
Date: Fri, 3 Jul 2026 00:26:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 17/19] camera: ship the FIDO CA HAL chain for cryptoeng
 device-attestation

The cryptoeng@1.0-service is extracted ;DISABLE_DEPS, so its two FIDO-CA
DT_NEEDED client stubs (fidoca-V1-ndk, fido2ca-V1-ndk) were never shipped
and the linker kills it every start -> ICryptoeng/default never registers.
An earlier change shipped the first missing dep (libqsee_keybox_ca.so); this
ships the rest of the chain so cryptoeng links, starts and registers, and
provides its FIDO backend:

  - the 2 FIDO CA HAL daemons (fidoca@1.0-service, fido2ca@1.0-service),
    their init rc, and their VINTF manifest fragments;
  - the 4 -ndk / -factory libs (fidoca/fido2ca-V1-ndk, libfido/libfido2
    _factory), 64- and 32-bit for stock parity;
  - the fidotap / fidoctap secure_ta blobs the daemons QSEECom-load.

All listed via proprietary-files.txt with ;DISABLE_DEPS exactly like the
cryptoeng and libqsee_keybox_ca entries; extract-utils regenerates the
module rules from each blob's real DT_NEEDED. No blob DT_NEEDED is edited.

This registers ICryptoeng and revives cryptoeng's consumers (Widevine/HDCP,
find-my-phone, PKI cert) and quiets the crash-loop. Note: hardware device
attestation returns 10048 (qsee_km_get_encapsulated_key) on an unlocked
bootloader -- a TrustZone lock-state gate -- so the cloud-attestation leg is
expected non-functional on LOS. This is device-tree completeness, not a
functional cloud feature (the AI editor was fixed separately via the OTA
version property).

Change-Id: I7301b4609273d298f97d44d8d1bd7245c2fb3fc8
